No,no.  You misunderstand the hardware climate back then.  Not
only did customers buy gear from us, they had gear from other
manufacturers and their own gear.  We weren't so arrogant back
then to dictate what customers had to use.  TOPS-10 provided
the sources so that any customer could implement whatever they
wanted.  Note that support of extensive software modifications
then had to be negotiated.
>
>These systems were for configuring the VAXes (and the reader gets the idea 
>that the amount of variability was really insane + the systems 
>were not quite modular: options affected other options).  One also 
>gets the impression that the PDP situation was even worse.

Not at all.  We didn't even try to do configurations for customers.
Some we couldn't know the configurations.  Our customers were
very sophisticated by the time they bought a system and we provided
training courses for the pieces they didn't know.

The one-size-fits-all Digital attitude didn't get started until the 80s.
There were valid reasons for this evolution of thinking.  I still
haven't figured out how to solve these problems well.

What you are talking about is how VAXland constructed the
software bill of lading when new hardware was bought by a new
customer.
<snip>

