I no longer know what you're talking about.  Vaxes were getting
more and more canned where the number of "hardware" options became
less.  There wasn't a choice of 20 different kinds of hard disk
pack drives.  There wasn't a choice of network gear.  Magtapes
were gone.  Disk geometries were off-loaded to the controller.
The biz had a mini mindset were the hardware was pretty much
consistent over all systems.  This was the beginning of 
automated manufacturing so customized tweaks were no longer
as necessary as they were in the days of hand-made computers.
Field service replaced boards rather than soldered to fix 
hardware bugs.  

