"Peter \"Firefly\" Lund" <firefly@diku.dk> writes:

> On Sun, 24 Sep 2006, Bob Willard wrote:
>
>> The notion that including non-shared MS780 memory would have
>> screwed SMP is moot, since VMS did not have any SMP capabilities
>> until well after the 780 timeframe.

> Sounds like you may be talking about different things when you write
> "SMP".

VMS got SMP at V5. The other problem was VMS used the CPU hardware as
part of the kernel syncronisation design, and that did not extend over
more than 1 CPU. See the AST Level Register et al in the ARM.

SMP for many came to mean CPU, Memory, and IO all pluged into a common
backplane. The model of CPU/IO pluging into a common memory with a
doorbell between CPUs dropped out of sight. But both 10/20 and 11 SMP
was the later. M-PLUS did not have coherant caches btw, it flushed the
cache as needed and the 11/44 in fact added a second set of valid bits
to the cache tag so you could swap sets and clear the unused set in
the background rather than having to stall while the entire cache is
invalidated. You really payed for that in the 11/7x, because the cache
and unibus both bussied out :( Which is also why unibus DMA on a 7x
sucked big time.


