prep@prep.synonet.com wrote:

> VMS got SMP at V5. The other problem was VMS used the CPU hardware as
> part of the kernel syncronisation design, and that did not extend over
> more than 1 CPU. See the AST Level Register et al in the ARM.

   The reference is to IPL, interrupt priority level.

   The original internal synchronization within (as it was then known) VMS used IPL.

   SMP continues to use IPL, though adds the use of the spinlock, a mechanism 
that uses a combination of IPLs for on-processor synchronization and the 
hardware level interlocked instructions for cross-processor synchronization.

   The OpenVMS Ask The Wizard area topics (2681), (6984) and (7383) have some 
general information on cache coherency and related, and touch on the interlocked 
instructions.

   In the more generic sense, SMP and ASMP configurations can have different 
speeds and feeds among the processors (in the simplest designs, this can be 
considered the backplane, but in many systems it's far more reminiscent of a 
network -- even as far as the processor and memory and I/O traffic on the 
"backplane" effectively being packetized and networked traffic), and different 
applications can benefit from one approach or the other.   Toward the other end 
of the spectrum of speeds and feeds, you have clustering as an SMP- or ASMP-like 
configuration, whether using reflexive memory or local memory or network 
hardware links or otherwise.

