Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply wrote:
> Since I have had no responses to my long post under a different title, 
> I'll try a short post with this title.
> 
> If I physically add an SBB (an RZ28 disk) disk to a running VAX system,
> in order to be able to use it, is the best (only) thing to do to issue
> the command 
> 
>    SYSGEN> AUTOCONFIGURE ALL
> 
> I've done this many times in the past with no problem.  Now, it froze
> the process doing it, caused members of shadow sets served by that node
> to be dismounted (after throwing the whole shadow sets into mount
> verification for a while first) and put a single disk (a CD in the
> internal CD drive) into permanent mount verification. 

   Bummer.  Most OpenVMS systems around don't have local hot-swap SCSI, and if 
the SCSI bus traffic should get interrupted when the brick arrives or departs 
the enclosure (and the SCSI bus itself), bad things can happen.

   I've seen what you report on occasion -- yes, I've also hot-swapped bricks as 
an expediency (on boxes that can be rebooted; non-production systems), but once 
in a while something gets nailed/wedged/locked and the process or the bus gets 
into an odd state.  And once in a while, SYSMAN can get itself snarled when a 
(local, non-hot-swap) disk arrives or departs "hot".

   There are SCSI controllers around which can selectively and transparently and 
temporarily quiesce the bus and can allow for disk hot-swaps, and SAS and SATA 
devices avoid the whole daisy-chain bus design.  SAS and SATA devices and 
controllers use a star topology, not a daisy-chain -- the host controller or the 
host drivers might still get befuddled on occasion, but it's rather easier to 
quiesce a bus with just one widget on it.

   But most SCSI controllers (and the SCSI host driver stack, too) can get 
befuddled when a signal transient nails whatever packet was in flight on the 
bus.  I've never dug around to see if it was the driver that was snarled, or the 
SCSI controller, or even something with the firmware in the brick having "gone 
insane" and locked up -- I knew I was being "bad" and I knew I "got caught"...







