Bob Willard wrote:

> Sorry, that was a mental lapse.  The master CPU handled all of the kernel
> code, including the I/O.

   The vast majority of kernel code, but not all.  There were various tweaks 
made through the (as it was then known) VAX/VMS V4.* range to avoid rescheduling 
over onto the primary where that was appropriate and safe and efficient -- and 
thus there were some sections of kernel that were safe.  Asymmetric 
Multiprocessing (ASMP) I/O on OpenVMS definitely required the full attention and 
involvement of the primary processor.

   Parts of OpenVMS I/O can still potentially involve some cycles on the primary 
processor, however, even within the symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) support on 
OpenVMS VAX V7.3.  (qv: "Fast Path", a mechanism that allows OpenVMS Alpha 
system managers and driver writers, for instance, to distribute this activity.)



