Rich Alderson wrote:
> "Peter \"Firefly\" Lund" <firefly@diku.dk> writes:
> 
>> On Thu, 14 Sep 2006, Bob Willard wrote:
> 
>>> It ran AMP, not SMP.  In fact, the next two multi-CPU VAXen also ran AMP,
>>> because the VMS ringleader was against SMP.
> 
>> Fair enough.  AMP or a kind of SMP with One Big Kernel Lock (so only one 
>> CPU executes inside the kernel at a time) is easier to get right.  You 
>> gotta start somewhere.
> 
> Yeah, but DEC already had the knowledge of how to do SMP well.  Unfortunately,
> the corporate culture, and especially the 32-bit subculture, embraced, nay,
> even celebrated, the NIH mentality.


   Bob, can you off-line me with the particular (engineering, I assume) 
ringleader you're remembering here?  I'm (simply) curious about that detail.

   Whether SMP is better or worse than ASMP is still a subject of debate, and 
there are products and derivatives in both spaces that are widely available. 
And it was and is quite clear from QUALIFY and related tools and from direct 
experience with the environment that ASMP works very well for some loads, and 
not so well for other ones.  Badly for some.  Which is obviously still true.

   The VAX-11/782 was the only VAX box that didn't make the jump to SMP, the 
next boxes -- those that also started out with ASMP -- were symmetric and 
capable of and made the jump to SMP support.

   As for the engineering, multiport-based memory and loosely-coupled processing 
is easier to implement and to maintain than is SMP.  And it's a safer bet.  It 
took a while to get the SMP boxes put together and working, and to get the (as 
it was then known) VAX/VMS modular kernel and SMP together and working -- and 
OpenVMS Engineering is still making adjustments and improvements to SMP.  V5.0 
was a massive change to the VAX/VMS kernel.

   I'll leave the early history around 16-bit, 32-bit and 36-bit computing, and 
around the various DIGITAL groups that were working in each of the areas, and 
the discussions of the various personalities involved, to others.  There have 
already been books written, and I expect there will be more written.






