And now that you have brought it up, I can relate my favorite Prime/Dec
anecdote.

In a previous life I worked for a major contractor.  We went head-to-head
with Dec on a bid.  We were bidding a Prime 850 and Dec opted to bid an,
as yet, non-existant Vax model.  The bid required the running of certain
benchmarks.  We showed up with boxes of fan-fold paper with the benchmark
results from the 850.  Dec showed up with an envelope containing a letter
stating "When this machine goes into production this is what the results
will be."  You would think this made us a shoe-in, wouldn't you?  Alas,
no.  The head of the propsal team stood up in one of the bid evaluation
meetings and stated: "I really don't care which one wins the proposal as
long as it says Vax on the front."  At that point we dropped out of the
process and let Dec have it as it was obvious we couldn't win even with
a far superior offering.

Ironicly, we did have the last laugh.  We later learned that the reason
this person wanted the Vax so bad was because he was planning on running
a bunch of free software packages from Kitt Peak Observatory.  The irony?
Dec bid and won the proposal with a VAX running VMS and all of this soft-
ware from Kitt Peak was for BSD Unix.  :-)
 
Those were some interesting times.  Especially when I hear people talking
like Dec had no real competition.  Performance-wise, Prime was much better
until bad management drove the company into the ground in a very short
period of time.

bill

