Peter "Firefly" Lund wrote:
> On Fri, 15 Sep 2006, Hoff Hoffman wrote:
> 
>>  Whether SMP is better or worse than ASMP is still a subject of 
>> debate, and
> 
> Is it?  I'd say that unless ones requirements are really weird then SMP 
> is always better -- *if* you can pull it off.

   There's usually more to computer design than just elegance.  There are also 
considerations around the associated costs and profits of the manufacturer and 
for the expected end-user, as almost all computers are commercial efforts. 
Trade-offs, all.  And as for the technology:

   Sometimes SMP wins.

   Sometimes clustering wins.

   Sometimes multiple parallel systems wins.

   SMP can win when the cost of the synchronization itself -- and the cost of 
implementing and maintaining the SMP itself, as well as the SMP-capable 
applications -- doesn't exceed the costs of the benefits derived.

   Asymmetric processing can be more effective and more appropriate under other 
application loads, whether for reasons of performance, implementation cost, or 
support.   Asymmetry can be a win in certain environments, and particularly for 
applications that are not particularly coupled -- the derivative products I'd 
mentioned (and that were chopped off the cited text) can include clustering 
(whether an OpenVMS Cluster, Linux Beowulf, or clustering otherwise, or 
message-passing environments)  and these constructs are obviously forms of 
asymmetric multiprocessing, and clustering and message passing seems to do quite 
well in some environments.

   Asymmetry can be a big win with protocol engines for instance, and there are 
may cases where off-loading the processing -- the OpenVMS lock manager can be 
offloaded and a processor within the SMP box can be removed from the SMP 
processor set and dedicated to that task -- as this can improve aggregate 
performance under certain specific application loads.

   And comparatively few SMP application environments scale upwards indefinitely 
-- all real-world application configurations I've encountered have eventually 
hit the proverbial wall, where adding additional processors into the 
configuration doesn't speed up the aggregate performance.  (qv: Amdahl's Law) At 
this threshold, either work within the target environment to address and to 
resolve the particular critical path(s) -- whether within the system or within 
the application -- is required, or the adoption of an asymmetric configuration 
-- clustering, for instance -- can become either necessary or expedient or more 
cost-effective.

   Where this threshold might be depends on the particular application and on 
the SMP implementation -- and on the relative costs of the other approaches.



