 Peter "Firefly" Lund wrote:
> I've heard that the VAX-11/782 was basically a two-cpu version of the
> VAX-11/780.

> Can anybody shed some light on how well the memory could keep of
> with two CPUs + I/O?

> The 11/780 had a cache or two, right?  A unified system cache that
> used physical addresses and an instruction cache that used virtual
> addresses.  The system cache could be called an L1 data cache and an
> L2 instruction cache with the virtual instruction cache (that lost
> its contents every time anybody said "booh!" to it) being the L1
> instruction cache.

> I suppose the 11/782 just duplicated the two caches?

> How did the cache coherence work?  Would any store by one CPU
> invalidate (parts of) the other CPU's cache(s)?

> Was the memory bandwidth sufficient for both CPUs?  Didn't
> invalidate-happy write-through caches burden the memory bus more
> than was desired?
 
> Were the customers happy with it?

A 782/784 was multiple complete 780s but with MA780 memory boxes
instead of MS780s. Each system had it's own caches, IO maps, IO
system.

The MA780s had multiple ports, and a set of tag bits for each memory
line. On a read from a port, that tag bit was set. When a write
happened, the set tag bits sent invalidation requests to all SBIs for
those ports. The idea was if you had not read the line, you did not
care about the invalidation so did not have to be interupted.

Problem 1 was that MA780s only came in the sizes `too small' and `way
too small' ;) so you really HAD to include MS780s and that screwed SMP
totally. The other was that you took a far performance hit from the MA
invalidate cycles.

Also NIH prevented anything from the 36-bit world, or from the
TRAX/M-PLUS work from getting a look in to VMS.

