On Sun, 24 Sep 2006, Bob Willard wrote:

> The notion that including non-shared MS780 memory would have screwed SMP
> is moot, since VMS did not have any SMP capabilities until well after
> the 780 timeframe.

Sounds like you may be talking about different things when you write 
"SMP".

I gather that the VMS versions available for 782 only ran the kernel on 
one CPU and had small stubs for the other(s) that would forward requests 
to the "kernel" CPU.

Probably a few system calls could run on all CPUs - for example the VMS 
counterparts of getpid() and gettimeofday() (UNIX calls that return the 
current process number and return the current date and time, 
respectively).

That's asymmetric vs symmetric multi-processing.

Another meaning could be regarding the memory semantics: is the memory 
properly coherent or not?

A third could be to contrast different kinds of wiring the memory but ways 
that are still coherent.  SMP here means that access to any piece of 
memory is symmetric, i.e. has the same bandwidth and latency no matter the 
requesting CPU and the physical memory address.  NUMA (non-uniform memory 
access) would be the most popular alternative - physics dictate that the 
way to get low latencies and high bandwidths is to put some memory close 
to each CPU.

-Peter


