Uh, in this context I believe I've consistently used SMP to mean Symmetric
Multi-Processing.  I was very sensitive to the difference between SMP and
AMP (or ASMP); hyper-sensitive even.

I have never heard SMP used to convey memory coherency.  And, Webopedia FWIW
uses SMP as I do, although it also shows SMP as an alternative to SNMP;
both are networking protocols.

The opposite of NUMA is UMA, not SMP.  {One of the ALPHAs had what I called
SemiUMA -- close enough that some folks tried to pass it off as UMA, but
not quite.}

But you're close to correct as for VMS on the 782.  When a process running
on the slave CPU attempted something delicate (e.g., I/O), the process
in toto was handed off to the master CPU for the asymmetric functions, and
then marked as being able to be run on either CPU.  Since the process was
completely in shared (albeit paged) memory, handing the process from one
CPU to the other was pretty efficient.
