> 
> As far as I know the VAX 11/782 was two VAX 11/780 (each with their own 
> memory subsystem) connected together with a shared memory buffer.  It 
> was more of a "cluster" than a true SMP (ie each 11/780 could not see 
> all of the other 11/780's memory...just what was in the shared buffer).
> 

Nope.  The 782 was a pair of 780 CPUs with a MS780 4MB shared memory box,
and all memory was shared.  Cache coherence was simple, since the CPUs
used write-through caching, with attendant invals.  Each CPU had its own
console, although the slave CPU's console was only useful for booting.
Memory bus bandwidth was not the 782's chief limiting factor.

It ran AMP, not SMP.  In fact, the next two multi-CPU VAXen also ran AMP,
because the VMS ringleader was against SMP.  AMP badly hurt performance on
many workloads, because every CMKRNL by a process running on the slave CPU
forced the process to be handed over to the master CPU.  Only the slave CPU
did I/O, which did avoid some sync problems, but hurt performance.

Some customers (mostly those with CPU-intensive apps) did like the 782,
but it was certainly not a giant hit.  The volume forecast was modest,
and total sales were 80% of the forecast.  Note that the 782, like the
785, was a stopgap VAX:  780 customers wanted more power, and the 790
(a/k/a 8600) slipped and slipped and slipped and ...

I sometimes say that I was the DRI for the first multi-CPU VAX, and also
for the first good multi-CPU VAX; sadly, those were different VAXen.  :-)
-- 
Cheers, Bob


