On Thu, 14 Sep 2006, Bob Willard wrote:

> Nope.  The 782 was a pair of 780 CPUs with a MS780 4MB shared memory box,
> and all memory was shared.  Cache coherence was simple, since the CPUs
> used write-through caching, with attendant invals.

DEC continued to use write-through caches for a long time, probably 
because they were so much easier to do cache coherence with.  But it did 
hurt performance, didn't it?

>  Each CPU had its own
> console, although the slave CPU's console was only useful for booting.
> Memory bus bandwidth was not the 782's chief limiting factor.
>
> It ran AMP, not SMP.  In fact, the next two multi-CPU VAXen also ran AMP,
> because the VMS ringleader was against SMP.

Fair enough.  AMP or a kind of SMP with One Big Kernel Lock (so only one 
CPU executes inside the kernel at a time) is easier to get right.  You 
gotta start somewhere.

>  AMP badly hurt performance on
> many workloads, because every CMKRNL by a process running on the slave CPU
> forced the process to be handed over to the master CPU.  Only the slave CPU
> did I/O, which did avoid some sync problems, but hurt performance.

The master executed the kernel code and the slave did the I/O?!?
Sounds like they would have to interrupt each other all the time to get 
anything done.

How was interprocessor interrupts done?

Didn't they wreak havoc on the virtual instruction cache?  I don't know 
how it was implemented on these early machines but on the VLSI machines 
(for which I've read the microcode and for some of them their specs), an 
