In article <Pine.LNX.4.61.0609140058170.9701@ask.diku.dk>, "Peter \"Firefly\" Lund" <firefly@diku.dk> writes:
> I've heard that the VAX-11/782 was basically a two-cpu version of the 
> VAX-11/780.

   The 11/782 was an asymetric multiprocessor with two 11/780 series
   CPUs, some processor specific memory, and some shared memory.

> Can anybody shed some light on how well the memory could keep of with two 
> CPUs + I/O?

   The shared memory subsystem was designed to be compatable with the
   VAX Architecure, which specifies how access is coordinated via
   things like interlocked instructions.

   In the 11/782, things like inner mode code were restricted to the
   primary CPU (thus asymetric), which made it a lot easier to deal
   with syncronisation within the OS.

> The 11/780 had a cache or two, right?  A unified system cache that used 
> physical addresses and an instruction cache that used virtual addresses. 
> The system cache could be called an L1 data cache and an L2 instruction 
> cache with the virtual instruction cache (that lost its contents every 
> time anybody said "booh!" to it) being the L1 instruction cache.

   The OS scheduled different processes on different CPUs.  Most
   addresses contain either read-only or process specific data.  Only
   shared writeable data need be coordinated.

> I suppose the 11/782 just duplicated the two caches?
> 
> How did the cache coherence work?  Would any store by one CPU 
> invalidate (parts of) the other CPU's cache(s)?

   I'll leave those details to the experts.

> Was the memory bandwidth sufficient for both CPUs?  Didn't 
> invalidate-happy write-through caches burden the memory bus more than was 
> desired?
> 
> Were the customers happy with it?

   DEC was happy with it, they had a lot of CPU bound work that ran
   nearly twice as fast.  Many customers got the same benefit. 
   Customers with I/O bound work needed a different solution.



