Peter "Firefly" Lund wrote:
> On Thu, 14 Sep 2006, Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
>>> Were the customers happy with it?
>>
>>   DEC was happy with it, they had a lot of CPU bound work that ran
>>   nearly twice as fast.  Many customers got the same benefit.
> 
> Huh?
> 
> Only stuff that could be parallelized, right?

   Yes.  Amdahl's Law does apply here, as it does in many other similar cases. 
Here paraphrasing (or even butchering) what Gene Amdahl was commenting on, the 
amount of the expected speed-up of parallelism is directly proportional to the 
amount of work that can run in parallel.   Obvious, yes -- but I've seen more 
than a few folks assume that adding additional processors into a single-threaded 
environment will be beneficial.  (Sometimes even adding processors into a 
multi-threaded environment can be, um, problematic.  But I digress.)

   Compute-bound stuff tended to run very nicely on the Atlas-class VAX-11/782, 
and was usually a good fit with the ASMP (Asymmetric Multiprocessing) design.

   The secondary could perform certain kernel mode code and there were certainly 
various task optimizations that checked settings and states before requesting 
rescheduling on the primary, but there were a large number of kernel operations 
that could only occur on the primary.

   You would probably see 1.8x (of a VAX-11/780) for these target cases and 
target environments, and sometimes better or worse performance depending on the 
particular details.

   The VAX-11/782 series system could potentially speed up source code 
compilations and links, for instance -- these have traditionally tended to 
perform a bunch of I/O up front to load the input into memory, then a whole lot 
of CPU time, then a whole lot of I/O to write out the results.  This could tend 
to parallelize.

   Eons ago, there was a DIGITAL tool known as QUALIFY, which looked at the 
system load on a VAX-11/780, and determined if the activities appeared likely to 
benefit from a VAX-11/782 ASMP upgrade.  Basically (obviously) QUALIFY profiled 
the system  activity and the typical existing system load, looking (and I've 
long forgotten the details) for CPU-bound stuff, and for stuff that would thrash.

> Single-process performance would not be better than on the 11/780, would 
> it?

   Single-stream performance could be worse, particularly when the secondary was 
thrashing.  If a task was scheduled onto the secondary that was ill-suited for 
independent operations and ASMP, it could be the classic toddler interrupting 
the parent.  QUALIFY would generally spot these, but every so often somebody 
would have (or add) a load that would thrash, and performance would suffer.

   The VAX-11/780 series system did not meet the baseline requirements for 
OpenVMS Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP) support, and existing systems upgraded 
to (as it was then) VAX/VMS V5.0 were split into two VAX-11/780 series boxes. 
SMP is a rather better solution than was SMP, though there are still various 
artifacts of asymmetry within the SMP design; there are still tasks that only 
run on a specified processor.

   After the upgrade to VAX/VMS V5.0 or later and after the hardware split, you 
could continue to use the MA780 multiport memory for communications between the 
two boxes.

   Various parts of the OpenVMS Galaxy design -- a more recent construction 
which obviously also involved shared memory "strung" between two OpenVMS hosts 
-- were very deliberately patterned after the old MA780 multiport constructs, too.



