On Thu, 14 Sep 2006, Rich Alderson wrote:

> Yeah, but DEC already had the knowledge of how to do SMP well.

They did?

Interesting.  I know next to nothing about the bewildering variety of 
PDP-nn machines and their operating systems.  Some of them /must/ have 
been interesting ;)

Which operating system(s) are you refering to?

> Unfortunately,
> the corporate culture, and especially the 32-bit subculture, embraced, nay,
> even celebrated, the NIH mentality.

But consolidation was long overdue in the seventies, wasn't it?  There 
were far too many different systems.

(And probably also far too many different VAX systems if you count all 
their options -- I'm going to ask some questions later about the 
configuration systems they used to handle all that complexity.  Maybe next 
week.)

Another question is: should they have dumped the PDP-11 instruction set? 
Yes, I know that the early VAX machines had a compatibility mode (in 
hardware), and that the VAX instruction set is "culturally compatible" but 
the fact is that they were two different instruction sets.  They probably 
should have kept them compatible, preferably without the need for a "mode 
bit".  Data General proved you could get quite far with that.  Intel did 
use a mode bit for their solution but the 32-bit instruction set is still 
clearly in the same family as the 16-bit instruction set.

But I'll get to that later.  I don't know enough about the PDP-11 to hold 
a relevant opinion.

-Peter



