Peter "Firefly" Lund wrote:

> Did VMS support threading, i.e. to "processes" sharing open files and 
> memory map?

   Yes, standard OpenVMS file processing and process-level operations and file 
sharing did and do apply.  Transparently.

   Asymmetric Multiprocessing (ASMP) was around for the VAX-11/782, and through 
for the versions of (as it was then known) VAX/VMS V4.* for the VAX 8300 and 
other related VAX multiprocessor systems.

   With VAX/VMS V5.0, Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP) was available and existing 
VAX systems such as the VAX 8300 -- boxes which met the SMP component-visibility 
requirements -- could operate fully symmetrically.  (The VAX-11/782 did not meet 
SMP requirements.)

> How did processes communicate on the 782?

   Using all the normal means (eg: traditional non-ASMP mailboxes, DECnet links 
and shared files, etc), or specifically using MA780 multiport shared memory or 
shared memory mailboxes.  If you poke around in even the current OpenVMS 
manuals, you'll find these shared-memory constructs and services still exist -- 
and as I mentioned in another reply, parts of the OpenVMS Galaxy environment 
very deliberately followed the old nomenclature and old interfaces where this 
was appropriate and feasible.  (We dug up our old manuals and references and 
notes, and...)

   And if you had the sufficient and obviously sufficiently working hardware 
examples, you could probably still get an MA780 to work, strung between a pair 
of VAX-11/780 series systems.  (These boxes were officially retired due to a 
(lack of) (blank) distribution media, but I know of no reason why these boxes 
would not bootstrap on OpenVMS VAX V7.3; on the current release.  They're no 
longer officially tested on newer releases, so there may well be problems I 
haven't heard of...)


